<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Yandabo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Yandabo was signed on 24 February 1826 by the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and the Konbaung kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a>. Burma gave up its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, Manipur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cachar">Cachar</a> and Jaintia, ceded Arakan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> to the British, paid an indemnity of one million pounds and accepted a British resident. It began British expansion into Burma and north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> had formally broken out on 5 March 1824. After their victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-danubyu">Battle of Danubyu</a> in April 1825, which killed the Burmese commander-in-chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-bandula">Maha Bandula</a>, the British held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, Rakhine, the Taninthayi coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and Manipur. Peace talks opened in September 1825 at Ngagyaungbinzeik, north of Pyay. The British demanded Rakhine, a Resident at the Court of Ava and an indemnity of two million pounds; the Burmese envoy, the lord of Kawlin, offered to give up Assam and Manipur and to cede Taninthayi but not Rakhine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The talks broke down. In November 1825 Burmese forces under Maha Ne Myo, largely Shan regiments led by their own sawbwas, nearly recaptured Pyay, but by early December British firepower had defeated them. Early in 1826 the British advanced on Ava, taking Pagan on 8 February and the village of Yandabo on 16 February, about four days' march from the capital.</p><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagyidaw">Bagyidaw</a> sent a delegation of one American, one English and two Burmese ministers to General Sir Archibald Campbell. There was no real negotiation: the Burmese accepted every demand. The treaty was signed by Campbell and by Maha Min Hla Kyaw Htin, Governor of Legaing. The Burmese paid a first installment of 250,000 pounds in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> bullion and released British prisoners of war; the British would stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> until the second installment was paid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The war was the longest and most expensive in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. Some 40,000 British and Indian troops took part, of whom 15,000 died, with an unknown but probably higher number of Burmese dead. Its cost contributed to an economic crisis that by 1833 had bankrupted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> agency houses and cost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> its monopoly of trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> the indemnity, equal to 10 million kyat when a villager in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> lived on one kyat a month, left the royal treasury bankrupt for years. The Court of Ava tried without success to recover the lost lands. The British fought two more wars against the weakened kingdom, in 1852 and 1885, and annexed the whole country by 1885.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Yandabo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British, at war with Burma from 1824, expelled the Burmese army, and by the <b>Treaty of Yandabo</b> in 1826 Burma renounced its claims to Assam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Yandabo</b> of February 1826 forced King Bagyidaw to cede Arakan and Tenasserim, renounce claims to Assam and Manipur and pay an indemnity of one million pounds sterling.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain took Arakan and Tenasserim after the First Anglo-Burmese War, settled by the <b>Treaty of Yandabo</b> in 1826.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-burma">British Burma</a></li></ul></section>
